# DRAFT — Caddy reverse proxy config for production.
# NOT deployed yet. Requires DOMAIN, API_DOMAIN, ACME_EMAIL to be set in the
# EC2 box's ./.env (see .env.prod.example). Caddy issues/renews Let's Encrypt
# certs automatically for these hostnames as long as DNS points at the box
# and 80/443 are reachable from the internet.
#
# Layout assumes two hostnames:
#   DOMAIN      -> the Next.js frontend (what users browse to)
#   API_DOMAIN  -> the FastAPI backend, needed because FastAdmin is mounted
#                  directly on the FastAPI app at /admin for direct browser
#                  access (see fastapi_backend/app/main.py), so the backend
#                  must be reachable from the public internet too, not just
#                  proxied internally through Next.js server actions.
#
# DOMAIN=app.buscaoficio.co / API_DOMAIN=api.buscaoficio.co. Both already have
# "A" records on Hostinger pointed at the EC2 instance's Elastic IP
# (44.207.170.68), confirmed resolving via `dig` on 2026-08-22.
#
# Google Sign-In needs no routing changes here: because each hostname proxies
# *everything* to one container (no path-based splitting), Next.js's own
# /api/auth/google/complete route handler (under DOMAIN) and FastAPI's
# /api/v1/auth/google/* routes (under API_DOMAIN) are both already reachable
# — there's no /api/* prefix collision to worry about between the two apps.

{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy frontend:3000
}

{$API_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy backend:8001
}
